            <div xml:id="d356">

                <pb facs="0547" n="447" sameAs="frus:frus1934v02/pg447"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">611.6231/415: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in
                        Germany</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Dodd</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1934-10-03T18:00:00-05:00">October 3, 1934—6
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>118. Your 191, October 2, noon. Commercial Attaché may say that the Trade
                    Agreements Act of 1934<anchor n="37" xml:id="d356-f1"/> gives the President
                    power to modify existing tariffs only in connection with reciprocal trade
                    agreements and that unless the barter arrangement under reference took the form
                    of a trade agreement there could be no lowering of duties on items
                    mentioned.</p>
                <p>As you know, no trade agreement with Germany under immediate consideration.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
